@page "/utils/training-set"
@using DotML.Playground.Common.Shared
@using DotML.Playground.Common.Shared.Tools

<TabView @ref=tabs>
    <Tab Title="Home">
        <div class="w3-center" style="vertical-align: top;">
            <button class="tool" @onclick="@(() => { tabs?.Activate(1); })">
                <b>Create</b>
                <img src="_content/DotML.Playground.Common/images/vector.svg">
                <p>
                    Create a new training set. This is useful when you want to create a new training set from scratch or from an existing one.
                </p>
            </button>
            <button class="tool" @onclick="@(() => { tabs?.Activate(2); })">
                <b>Merge</b>
                <img src="_content/DotML.Playground.Common/images/merge.svg">
                <p>
                    Merge multiple training sets into one. This is useful when you have multiple training sets that you want to combine into one for training a model.
                </p>
            </button>
            <button class="tool" @onclick="@(() => { tabs?.Activate(3); })">
                <b>Inspect</b>
                <img src="_content/DotML.Playground.Common/images/inspect.svg">
                <p>
                    Inspect a training set. This is useful when you want to see the contents of a training set or to check if it is valid.
                </p>
            </button>
        </div>
        <div class="w3-container">
            <p>
                The above tools are used to create, merge, and inspect training sets. A training set is a collection of data vectors that is used to train a machine learning model. The tools are designed to be easy to use and to provide a simple interface for creating, merging, and inspecting training sets.
            </p>
            <p>
                Training sets are saved in a custom binary format designed to be easy to read and write regardless of the host language. The description below is intended as a reference to writing your own parser for the format.
            </p>
            <p>
                <b>Header</b>
                <p>
The first 3 bytes of file should be the ascii characters 'v', 'e', and 'c' as in the word "vector". If a file doesn't start with these characters it is not considered to be a binary training set.
This is then followed by a single byte representing what kind of value the vectors contain, 8 bytes representing a scaling factor, 4 bytes for the number of possible output vectors and 4 bytes for the number of input vectors.
<table class="w3-table">
	<thead>
		<th>Size (bytes)</th>
		<th>Type</th>
		<th>Description</th>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td>3</td>
			<td>-</td>
			<td>The characters 'vec'.</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>1</td>
			<td>byte</td>
			<td>Type of element stored in each vector. One of: U8 = 0b0001_0000, U16 = 0b0001_0001, U32 = 0b0001_0010, U64 = 0b0001_0011, I8 = 0b0010_0000, I16 = 0b0010_0001, I32 = 0b0010_0010, I64 = 0b0010_0011, F16 = 0b0100_0001, F32 = 0b0100_0010, F64 = 0b0100_0011.</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>8</td>
			<td>double</td>
			<td>Floating point value to scale all read input / output vectors by.</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>4</td>
			<td>int</td>
			<td>Number of distinct output vectors.</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>4</td>
			<td>int</td>
			<td>Number of distinct input vectors.</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
                </p>
            </p>
            <p>
                <b>Output Vectors</b>
                <p>
Subsequently the output vectors are listed. Since we already know how many output vectors exist (from the header) We simply need to read that many vectors from the stream.
Each vector starts with an integer representing the number of elements followed by those elements. The Element Type provided in the header controls how the elements are read from the stream. For instance if the header says the elements are I32 then each element is a 4 byte integer stored in little-endian order.
<table class="w3-table">
	<thead>
		<th>Size (bytes)</th>
		<th>Type</th>
		<th>Description</th>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td>4</td>
			<td>int</td>
			<td>Count of elements in the vector</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>varies (1-8 bytes)</td>
			<td>Element Type</td>
			<td>An individual element of the vector, read in row-major order</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>...</td>
			<td>...</td>
			<td>...</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
                </p>
            </p>
            <p>
                <b>Input Vectors</b>
                <p>
Last to be read are the input vectors, these are stored almost identically to the output vectors, but are prefixed by an additional 32bit integer that links the input vector to its associated output vector. Indices are 0-based.
<table class="w3-table">
	<thead>
		<th>Size (bytes)</th>
		<th>Type</th>
		<th>Description</th>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td>4</td>
			<td>int</td>
			<td>Index of this input's ouput vector in the output vector list</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>4</td>
			<td>int</td>
			<td>Count of elements in the vector</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>varies (1-8 bytes)</td>
			<td>Element Type</td>
			<td>An individual element of the vector, read in row-major order</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>...</td>
			<td>...</td>
			<td>...</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
                </p>
            </p
        </div>
    </Tab>
    <Tab Title="Create">

    </Tab>
    <Tab Title="Merge">
        <TrainingSetMerge></TrainingSetMerge>
    </Tab>
    <Tab Title="Inspect">
		<TrainingSetInspect></TrainingSetInspect>
    </Tab>
</TabView>

@code {
    private TabView? tabs;
}